# Package install scripts are off for this project.
#
# scripts/deploy-api.sh runs `npm ci --omit=dev` on the droplet as www-data, on
# the same box that serves the team's WordPress and MySQL. Without this, any
# package in the production closure that gains a postinstall — its own, or one
# slipped into a transitive dependency — executes arbitrary code as the web user
# on that host. That is the whole supply-chain attack: you do not have to
# compromise us, only something we depend on.
#
# This file is rsync'd to /opt/ib-api by scripts/deploy-api.sh so the remote
# install reads it too, and covers a `npm ci` run by hand on the droplet. The
# deploy also passes --ignore-scripts explicitly, so the protection does not
# quietly depend on this file having shipped.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Does this break local development? It was checked, not assumed.
#
# Five packages in package-lock.json carry hasInstallScript, and none of them
# need their script to work:
#
#   esbuild        prod, pulled in by tsx. lib/main.js resolves
#                  @esbuild/<platform> at runtime; the postinstall only swaps
#                  the JS bin shim for the native binary to save a few ms.
#   sharp          prod, optional, used by next for image optimisation. Since
#                  0.33 it loads @img/sharp-<platform>/sharp.node directly, and
#                  its install script (`node install/check.js`) only builds from
#                  source when a GLOBAL libvips is present.
#   unrs-resolver  dev, via eslint-config-next. Loads its
#                  @unrs/resolver-binding-<platform> package at runtime.
#   fsevents       optional, macOS only, and irrelevant on the Linux droplet.
#   playwright's fsevents   same.
#
# Verified 2026-09-18 with a clean `npm ci` in a scratch directory with this
# file in place: the esbuild postinstall demonstrably did not run (bin/esbuild
# was still the JS shim, not a Mach-O binary) and sharp, the esbuild JS API, the
# esbuild CLI, tsx and unrs-resolver all still worked — under both the full
# install and --omit=dev. Playwright browsers are unaffected: they come from the
# explicit `npm run test:e2e:install`, not from an install script.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One consequence to know about, because npm does not warn you.
#
# npm applies ignore-scripts to the pre/post hooks of `npm run` as well. This
# repo has exactly one such hook, `postbuild:static`, so `npm run build:static`
# no longer runs tests/rendered-pages.test.ts, tests/link-audit.test.ts and
# scripts/audit-links.mjs afterwards.
#
# Nothing that actually ships loses a gate: scripts/deploy.sh does not go
# through `npm run build:static` — it calls `npx next build` and then runs
# audit-links.mjs and those same two test files itself. But if you build the
# static site by hand with `npm run build:static`, run the audit yourself:
#
#   npx vitest run tests/rendered-pages.test.ts tests/link-audit.test.ts
#   node scripts/audit-links.mjs out --mode static --base /resources
#
# If a future dependency genuinely needs its install script, do not delete the
# line below. Rebuild that one package — `npm rebuild <pkg> --ignore-scripts=false`
# — and record here why it needs it.
ignore-scripts=true

# Never put a registry auth token in this file. It is committed to git, and
# scripts/deploy-api.sh copies it to the droplet.
